TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Preliminary Report (Part 1)","overview":"Plan a simple space mission that benefits Australia and present preliminary design work to guide the full mission assignment. Explain the mission concept and rationale, plan how the assignment will be completed, identify information sources and software, and show evidence of technical progress."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Mission concept and rationale — What will it do, who are the users, why is it needed?","weight":40,"max":40,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"A clear and complete description of mission goals, objectives, and requirements. Clearly relates required mission function to users and their needs. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Sufficient description of mission goals, objectives, and requirements. Identification of users and their needs. Descriptions feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"A poor description with mission goals, objectives and requirements unclear. Insufficient identification of users and their needs."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Project plan — How you plan to complete the assignment, including either a Gantt chart or a timetable and information sources you plan to use","weight":30,"max":30,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The plan for project completion is robust and has provision for project variations and contingencies. The plan fits within the narrative set out by the mission concept – the student makes clear why the plan is developed this way in the context of the mission. Appropriate references to information sources are included."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Plan is presented but lacks some detail. However, the plan is enough to believe that the project is feasible."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Plan is not included or does not have sufficient detail to demonstrate that the project can be completed."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Preliminary progress — Technical content and evidence of sufficient work done to date","weight":10,"max":10,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Good preliminary set of results included with brief description and/or interpretation. Clearly on track for completion."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Minor work completed on the project. Results description or interpretation may be insufficient."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful work completed."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":20,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical structure. The report is very easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Mission Concept & Rationale"},{"id":"R2","met":"partial","where":"Project Plan & Preliminary Progress"},{"id":"R3","met":"yes","where":"Project Plan & Preliminary Progress"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Image 1"},{"id":"R5","met":"partial","where":"Mission Concept & Rationale"},{"id":"R6","met":"no","where":"Project Plan & Preliminary Progress"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Orbit Specifics","issue":"A 600 km altitude orbit cannot be geostationary or remain fixed over the reef; geostationary orbit is approximately 35,786 km above Earth’s equator. The stated orbit therefore does not support the claimed continuous fixed view."},{"where":"Mission Concept & Rationale","issue":"The text implies ocean acidification causes coral bleaching. Warming and thermal stress are the principal direct drivers of mass bleaching; acidification is a separate major stressor and may compound harm."},{"where":"Mission Concept & Rationale","issue":"Claims that bleaching killed 60% of the reef’s coral in 2016 and another 50% in 2017 are not adequately qualified or supported. They appear to conflate bleaching, affected area, and coral mortality."},{"where":"Mission Concept & Rationale","issue":"Near-real-time coverage and satellite detection of seafloor colour change are asserted without a sensor, orbit, revisit, water-depth, turbidity, cloud, or communications feasibility analysis. A single low-Earth-orbit satellite would not provide continuous coverage."},{"where":"Project Plan & Preliminary Progress","issue":"No preliminary calculations, simulation results, or other technical design outputs are presented; the stated research and tutorials do not demonstrate technical progress on the mission design."}],"overall":"The mission is relevant and its users and rationale are identifiable, with a workable high-level schedule and STK selection. The submission lacks technical results and a sufficiently specified information-source plan. The proposed 600 km geostationary orbit is a fundamental error, and the sensing and near-real-time claims need feasibility analysis.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Adequate","mark":6.0,"scale":10.0,"points":24.0,"max":40.0,"reasoning":"The report establishes a relevant Australian mission, identifies conservation and marine scientists as users, and explains the intended benefit. However, mission objectives and requirements remain broad: there are no quantified coverage, revisit, resolution, or data-delivery requirements.","evidence":[{"where":"Mission Concept & Rationale","quote":"A near-real-time relay to monitoring stations on the ground would allow marine and conservation scientists to more accurately predict and react to coral bleaching events."},{"where":"Mission Concept & Rationale","quote":"This tool could be applied to a new satellite over the Great Barrier Reef."}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":15.0,"max":30.0,"reasoning":"A week-by-week Gantt chart and task descriptions make the project plan plausible and connect it to launch, orbit, communications, and design work. Contingencies are absent, dependencies and milestones are limited, and planned information sources are not specified in sufficient detail.","evidence":[{"where":"Image 1","quote":""},{"where":"Project Plan & Preliminary Progress","quote":"The goal is to brainstorm and research as many possible strategies for the satellite’s launch/orbit and then gradually cut and refine them over the next few weeks"}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Deficient","mark":2.0,"scale":10.0,"points":2.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The report describes background research and tutorials, but gives no technical results, calculations, or demonstrated STK outputs. Its only preliminary orbit selection is physically incorrect: 600 km altitude is not geostationary.","evidence":[{"where":"Orbit Specifics","quote":"My current choice is a geostationary orbit at an altitude of 600 km, which keeps the satellite fixed above the reef at all times."},{"where":"Project Plan & Preliminary Progress","quote":"completed STK tutorials 1, 2, 3 & 4."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":6.0,"scale":10.0,"points":12.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"The report has clear headings, readable prose, and a legible, labelled Gantt chart. Presentation is generally understandable, but references are supplied as bare URLs rather than complete citations, the Gantt-chart deadline markers are not explained by date, and some scientific claims are imprecise.","evidence":[{"where":"Project Plan & Preliminary Progress","quote":"Detailed task descriptions can be found on the next page."},{"where":"Mission Concept & Rationale","quote":"https://www.deepreef.org/projects/48-depth-model-gbr.html"}]}]}
